:: Foucault-Reference-Main {"position":"1150,575","size":"100,100"}
## REFERENCE: FOUCAULT - "THE EYE OF POWER"

### AUTHOR'S ARGUMENT:
The Panopticon prison design reveals how modern power operates not through 
physical force but through visibility and surveillance—where the possibility 
of being watched makes people regulate their own behavior, creating a 
self-sustaining system of control that extends far beyond prisons into 
schools, hospitals, factories, and digital platforms.

### KEY POINTS:
- The Panopticon creates power through asymmetric visibility—guards can see 
  prisoners but prisoners can't see guards, so inmates assume they're always 
  watched and discipline themselves
- Modern disciplinary power is "self-amplifying" through "successive 
  transformations"—it constantly adapts and changes, making it both 
  precarious and inventive, unlike static monarchical power
- Resistance always exists—people find ways to "slip through the net" and 
  create connection despite surveillance -- example: prisoners sharing 
  cigarettes through walls
- This model extends everywhere: schools organized around teacher visibility, 
  hospitals with central nursing stations, factories with supervisor 
  oversight, digital platforms with data collection

### KEY TERMS/CONCEPTS:
- **Panopticon** (Bentham, 1791): Circular prison with central observation 
  tower. Prisoners backlit and visible; tower inhabitants invisible. "Just a 
  gaze. An inspecting gaze, a gaze which each individual under its weight 
  will end by interiorising to the point that he is his own overseer"
- **Disciplinary Power** vs. Monarchical Power: Discipline operates through 
  surveillance/documentation (cheap, efficient) vs. monarchy through 
  spectacle/violence (expensive, dramatic). Discipline is "self-amplifying" 
  and transforms constantly
- **Opacity** (my note): Systems that keep their operations invisible while 
  making subjects visible. Platform algorithms, institutional policies, 
  surveillance infrastructure—you can't see how they work or when they 
  change
- **Resistance** (Perrot's question): "Were there revolts against the gaze?" 
  Always. Workers refused company housing, maintained craft knowledge, found 
  ways to connect despite separation

### KEY QUOTATIONS:
- "Just a gaze. An inspecting gaze, a gaze which each individual under its 
  weight will end by interiorising to the point that he is his own overseer, 
  each individual thus exercising this surveillance over, and against, 
  himself" (p. 155)
- "The power of the bourgeoisie is self-amplifying, in a mode not of 
  conservation but of successive transformations...Hence both its 
  precariousness and its supple inventiveness" (p. 165)
- "They didn't realise that there would always be ways of slipping through 
  their net, or that resistances would have a role to play" (p. 165)
- "Are we in the aforementioned glass cell?" (my note)

### STRENGTHS:
- Explains how control works WITHOUT physical force—essential for 
  understanding digital surveillance
- Shows how architecture and system design create power relations (relevant 
  for interface design, platform studies)
- Accounts for constant change/adaptation in power systems (platform 
  algorithms that update constantly)
- Insists on resistance—people aren't passive subjects

### LIMITATIONS:
- Can be totalizing—everything becomes surveillance, hard to imagine outside
- Doesn't fully account for pleasure/desire in being watched (social media 
  performance)
- Written pre-internet; needs extension for algorithmic control
- Can minimize agency—people DO find ways to resist, create, connect

### RELATION TO T&T RESEARCH:
- **Digital Humanities**: Databases and platforms as panoptic—who can see 
  what data?
- **Digital Media**: Streaming platforms, games, social media all involve 
  asymmetric visibility
- **EPIC**: Publishing platforms control what's visible; editors surveil 
  submissions
- **Public History**: Archives decide what gets preserved/made visible
- **Rhetoric & Composition**: LMS systems (instructor sees everything, 
  students see little)

### CONNECTIONS TO OTHER AUTHORS:
- **Ricoeur**: CONTRAST—Foucault emphasizes CONTROL through fixation 
  (surveillance inscribes power), Ricoeur emphasizes LIBERATION through 
  fixation (texts enable appropriation). Same mechanism, opposite valences
- **Oulipo**: CRITICAL CONTRAST—Oulipian systems stay FIXED and TRANSPARENT 
  vs. Foucault's "self-amplifying power through successive transformations." 
  Oulipo resists the opacity that enables control
- **Laurel**: System design creates possibility spaces (Laurel) vs. control 
  mechanisms (Foucault). Same architectural principle, different outcomes

### MY CONNECTIONS TO T&T WORK:
- Poetry synthesizer: Transparent, fixed constraints vs. opaque corporate 
  algorithms (resisting panoptic control of language)
- LAMOS: Documenting failed formats—what gets "seen" in archives vs. what 
  disappears
- Francoism: Using Jess Franco (exploitation cinema) to examine how cultural 
  hierarchies determine visibility
- Teaching: LMS platforms create asymmetric visibility (I see all student 
  work, they don't see each other or the full picture)

[[Back to pathway|Group1-Intro]]


:: Foucault-Reference-Panopticon {"position":"1150,700","size":"100,100"}
## REFERENCE: THE PANOPTICON CONCEPT

### CONCEPT:
Jeremy Bentham's 1791 prison design becomes Foucault's model for how modern 
power operates through visibility rather than violence.

### THE ARCHITECTURE:
- Circular building with cells around perimeter
- Central observation tower in middle
- Backlighting makes prisoners always visible to tower
- Prisoners CANNOT see into tower—don't know if they're being watched
- Result: Prisoners assume constant surveillance, regulate their own behavior

### FOUCAULT'S KEY INSIGHT (p. 155):
"Just a gaze...each individual under its weight will end by interiorising 
to the point that he is his own overseer, each individual thus exercising 
this surveillance over, and against, himself."

The guard might not even be there. The POSSIBILITY of being watched is 
enough. Power becomes automatic, self-sustaining.

### THE EFFICIENCY:
Old model (monarchy): Power requires spectacle, violence, constant force. 
Expensive. Concentrated in sovereign's body.

New model (discipline): Power requires only visibility. Cheap. Dispersed 
through architecture.

Quote (p. 158): "In contrast to that you have the system of surveillance, 
which on the contrary involves very little expense. There is no need for 
arms, physical violence, material constraints. Just a gaze."

### BEYOND PRISONS:
Foucault traces this model everywhere:

**Schools** (p. 147):
Military School Paris (1751)—rows of students facing teacher at front. 
Architecture ensures all students visible, teacher position commands view. 
Modern classrooms still use this.

**Hospitals**:
Nursing stations with sight lines to all beds. Patient visibility enables 
monitoring. Medical gaze as control.

**Factories**:
Supervisors overlooking factory floor. Worker visibility ensures 
productivity. Managers see everything; workers see only their station.

**Workshops**:
Master craftsmen monitoring apprentices. Visibility ensures quality control 
and knowledge transmission (though workers resisted this—maintained craft 
secrets).

### DIGITAL EXTENSIONS (my notes):
- Social media platforms (you're visible through posts/data; algorithms 
  invisible)
- Workplace monitoring (keystroke logging, productivity tracking, Slack 
  "active" status)
- CCTV cameras (might not be recording, but you assume they are)
- Ring doorbells (neighbors might be watching)
- LMS platforms (professor sees all student activity)
- Platform analytics (creators perform for invisible metrics)
- Body cameras (police/public assume constant recording)

### MY NOTE: "Are we in the aforementioned glass cell?"

We ARE. Platform architecture, institutional monitoring, data collection—
all create asymmetric visibility where WE are seen but can't see how 
systems work.

THE PANOPTICON IS INTERACTIVE:
It's not just that you're watched—it's that you KNOW you might be watched, 
so you change your behavior. You perform for the invisible gaze.

Connection to social media: You curate your posts assuming someone's 
watching. The algorithm determines what's visible to whom, but you can't 
see the algorithm. Perfect panopticism.

CONNECTION TO RICOEUR:
Panopticon involves FIXATION (Ricoeur)—your actions get "recorded" 
(surveillance logs, data, documentation). But Foucault emphasizes CONTROL 
vs. Ricoeur's LIBERATION. Same fixation mechanism, opposite outcomes.

CRITICAL QUESTION:
Can we design ANTI-PANOPTIC systems? What would those look like?
- End-to-end encryption (Signal, Tor)
- Federated platforms (Mastodon—no central authority)
- Anonymous systems (no surveillance possible)
- Transparent algorithms (open-source, auditable)

But: These are hard to scale, hard to fund, require technical knowledge. 
Panopticon is efficient because it's cheap and self-sustaining.

[[Back to pathway|Group1-Intro]]
[[Back to discussion|Foucault-Discussion-1]]


:: Foucault-Reference-Resistance {"position":"1150,950","size":"100,100"}
## REFERENCE: RESISTANCE AND "SLIPPING THROUGH THE NET"

### FOUCAULT'S INSISTENCE:
People are NOT passive subjects. Where there is power, there is resistance.

Perrot's question (p. 165): "Were there revolts against the gaze?"

Foucault's answer: Always. "They didn't realise that there would always be 
ways of slipping through their net, or that resistances would have a role 
to play."

### HISTORICAL RESISTANCE:

**Workers refusing company housing (Cités Ouvrières):**
Companies built housing to extend surveillance beyond work hours. Workers 
REFUSED—maintained separate housing to preserve autonomy. Resistance 
through refusal.

**Craft knowledge as counter-power:**
Skilled workers maintained specialized knowledge that managers couldn't 
access. This gave workers power even under surveillance. Management 
responded by mechanizing those functions to eliminate worker control—but 
this shows resistance was EFFECTIVE enough to require response.

**Genet's "Un Chant d'Amour" (my note):**
Film about prisoners separated by thick stone walls. They share a cigarette 
through a straw threaded through a hole in the wall. Find erotic connection 
THROUGH the architecture designed to separate them.

This is "queering the prison"—using the structure itself as site of 
intimacy rather than just control. Not escaping surveillance but creating 
meaning DESPITE it. Notable, however, is that these subversive acts of masturbation and erotic connection are still under the watchful eye of the prison guard, who takes joy in watching these acts as he patrols his inmates.

### CONTEMPORARY RESISTANCE STRATEGIES:

**Technologies of Opacity:**
- End-to-end encryption (Signal, Tor)—surveillance can't penetrate
- VPNs—hide location and activity from ISPs
- Anonymous browsing (Tor, privacy browsers)
- Burner phones/accounts—temporary identities

**Circumventing Gatekeepers:**
- Sci-Hub, Library Genesis—making paywalled knowledge accessible
- Piracy—accessing restricted media
- Shadow libraries—preserving materials institutions won't
- Bypassing geographic restrictions (VPNs for region-locked content)

**Using Platforms Against Intended Purpose:**
- Twitter threading to exceed character limits (platform wants concision)
- Instagram Stories for ephemeral content (resisting permanent archive)
- Finstas—separating performed public self from private self
- Discord servers as alternative communities outside mainstream platform 
  control

**Building Alternatives:**
- Open-source software (Linux, LibreOffice—transparent, modifiable)
- Federated platforms (Mastodon—no central authority to surveil)
- Community-owned infrastructure (mesh networks, local servers)
- Zines, independent publishing (outside institutional gatekeeping)

**Practices of Opacity:**
- Going off-grid (refusing to participate)
- Cash instead of cards (untraceable transactions)
- Refusing social media presence
- Using multiple identities/pseudonyms
- Lying to data collection systems

**Policy Resistance:**
- GDPR (right to be forgotten, data portability)
- Demands for algorithmic transparency
- Antitrust actions against platform monopolies
- Right-to-repair movements (resisting planned obsolescence)

### MY T&T EXAMPLES:

**Poetry Synthesizer:**
Open-source hardware (Raspberry Pi), transparent algorithms (visible Markov 
chains), modifiable constraints. Anyone can see how it works, build their 
own, change the rules. ANTI-panoptic creative tool.

**Using Jess Franco Films (Francoism):**
Working with exploitation cinema that dominant culture renders invisible. 
Making visible what's excluded from canonical hierarchies. Resisting 
cultural surveillance that determines what counts as "serious."

**LAMOS Project:**
Documenting failed formats, ephemeral media, things that disappear. 
Resisting archival logics that determine what's preserved/visible.

**Teaching with Open Materials:**
Using open-access texts, Creative Commons resources. Resisting textbook 
monopolies and paywalls that determine who can access knowledge.

### THE CRITICAL QUESTION:
Can we ESCAPE panoptic systems in 2026?

**Pessimistic View:**
No. Surveillance is too diffuse, too cheap, too efficient. Even resistance 
tools (Tor, Signal) require technical knowledge and privilege. Going 
off-grid means losing access to essential services. Opting out isn't 
really possible.

**Optimistic View:**
Yes—through constant, creative, partial resistance. Genet's model: find 
connection THROUGH the architecture. Build alternatives. Use systems 
against themselves. "Slip through the net."

**Foucault's View (probably):**
Both. Resistance is always partial, always compromised. But it's also 
always present, always creative, always finding new ways to operate. Power 
and resistance exist together.

### CONNECTION TO OULIPO:
Transparent constraint-based systems RESIST panoptic control. If you can 
see the rules, replicate them, modify them—power isn't opaque and 
self-amplifying. Democratic systems vs. controlling ones.

### CONNECTION TO RICOEUR:
Appropriation requires seeing how the text works. Opaque systems prevent 
appropriation—you can't make meaning your own if you can't see how meaning 
is made. Resistance enables appropriation.

