:: Group1-Intro {"position":"1100,425","size":"100,100"}
**MICHEL FOUCAULT: THE EYE OF POWER**

In this 1980 interview, Foucault discusses the Panopticon—a prison design 
where guards can see all inmates but inmates can't see guards. But he's 
really talking about how modern power works.

**The Core Move:**
Visibility becomes a mechanism of control. When you MIGHT be watched at 
any moment, you discipline yourself. Power becomes automatic, self-sustaining.

**Key Concepts:**

**Panopticism** = Power through potential surveillance
- You can't see if you're being watched
- So you assume you ARE watched
- So you police your own behavior
- Guard becomes unnecessary

**Architecture as Power:**
Space isn't neutral—design creates social relations. The Panopticon builds 
power into the walls.

**Beyond Prisons:**
Foucault argues this model extends everywhere: schools (rows facing teacher), 
hospitals (nursing stations observing wards), factories (supervisors 
overlooking floor), digital platforms (data collection you can't see).

**Why This Matters for T&T:**
- Algorithmic systems often work like panopticons (invisible, automated, 
  self-disciplining)
- Platform architecture creates power relations (who sees what? who knows 
  they're being watched?)
- BUT: Foucault also discusses RESISTANCE—systems designed to resist 
  surveillance

**Your Three Discussions:**

1. **Visibility & Control** - How does being seen (or thinking you're seen) 
   change behavior?

